19. Exact interaction rules
These rules should be shared across every module.
Buttons
Every button must have five states:
1. Default.
2. Hover.
3. Pressed.
4. Disabled.
5. Loading.
Primary buttons:
- Turquoise background.
- White text.
- Medium font weight.
- 44px minimum height.
- 10px corner radius.
Secondary buttons:
- White background.
- Light seafoam border.
- Deep ocean text.
Danger buttons:
- Muted coral/red.
- Used only for destructive actions.
Button labels should describe the action. Use Save Customer, not OK, for example.
Forms
Every field should support:
- Empty.
- Focused.
- Filled.
- Invalid.
- Disabled.
- Read-only.
Validation should occur when appropriate, such as after leaving a field or submitting the form. Do not bombard users with errors before they've had a chance to type.
Dropdowns
Dropdowns should:
- Open below the field.
- Have a search option when there are many items.
- Support keyboard navigation.
- Show the selected item.
- Close when the user clicks outside.
- Display a clear empty state if there are no choices.
Confirmation dialogs
Use confirmation dialogs for:
- Deactivating customers.
- Reversing payments.
- Cancelling invoices.
- Removing staff accounts.
- Changing important tariff configurations.
Example:

Deactivate customer?

This will prevent the customer account from being used for new transactions. Historical invoices and payments will remain available.

Buttons:
- Cancel.
- Deactivate Customer.
Search
Global search should be able to find records the user is authorised to see:
- Customers.
- Account numbers.
- Invoices.
- Receipts.
- Meter serial numbers.
- Fault references.
Search results should be grouped by category.

Never expose sensitive records through search if the user does not have permission to view them.

20. Animation system — exact design rules
Use Motion for React for more complex transitions and CSS transitions for simple colour and shadow changes.
Page transition
When changing pages:
- Old page fades out.
- New page fades in.
- New content moves up by approximately 8px.
- Duration: 180–250ms.
Card entrance
Dashboard cards appear in a slight stagger:
- First card.
- Second card.
- Third card.
- Fourth card.
The stagger should be subtle. Do not delay the entire dashboard while the animation plays.
Pip loading animation
Pip:
- Moves gently upward.
- Returns to its original position.
- A circular ripple expands underneath.
- Ripple fades out.
- Animation repeats while the data request is pending.
Water-inspired progress
Progress bars should fill smoothly, with a gentle wave-like edge if practical.

Use this for:
- Meter-reading completion.
- Work-order progress.
- Payment allocation progress.
Success animation
After a successful operation:
- Pip performs one small bounce.
- A small ripple appears.
- A success message slides in.
- The interface updates with the newly saved record.
Important animation restriction
Animations must never:
- Prevent form submission.
- Delay navigation unnecessarily.
- Make users wait for data.
- Hide important error messages.
- Run excessively on every screen.
Support reduced-motion preferences.

21. Database and business rules that must never be overlooked
These are the rules that make the application a real information system rather than a visual prototype.
Customer records
- Account numbers must be unique.
- Customer details must be validated.
- A customer can have multiple connections.
- Deactivation must preserve history.
Connections and meters
- A meter belongs to a connection.
- Meter replacements must preserve old readings.
- A connection can have a history of meter installations.
- Only active and eligible connections should enter the normal billing process.
Meter readings
- Readings must be associated with a meter.
- Readings must have a date and reading period.
- Consumption must be calculated consistently.
- Duplicate period submissions must be prevented.
- Approved readings are used for final billing.
Billing
- Tariffs have effective dates.
- Old invoices retain their original calculation details.
- An invoice cannot be generated twice for the same billing period and connection.
- Invoice balances must reflect payments, adjustments and credits.
Payments
- Payments must have unique transaction or receipt references.
- A payment can be allocated to one or more invoices.
- Partial payments must work.
- Reversals must be traceable.
- Payment records should never simply disappear.
Faults
- Every fault gets a unique reference.
- Every status change has a timestamp and user.
- Work orders link to their corresponding faults.
- Technicians can only update jobs they are authorised to manage.
Reports
- Report figures must come from stored records.
- Reports must respect the selected date range.
- Financial reports must use consistent accounting rules.
- Users should only see information their roles permit.

